## Retro

### Active cycle (2026-04-25 — root revision: brain.aide path move + launcher rename + schema collapse)

This spec is the canonical author of the scaffolded brain.aide bytes. When the root revises the schema, the launcher, or the file path, this spec is the single point where the new shape lands in the install pipeline — and accordingly absorbs the most cascade. Five interlocking decisions in one root revision means five edits land here: the file path moves to `.aide/config/brain.aide`, the launcher in the canonical default becomes `@bitbonsai/mcpvault`, the frontmatter schema collapses to `name` + `mcpServerConfig.{command, args}` only, the `connector` field is renamed to `name`, and the `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` helper either renames or shifts identity to reflect the new launcher.

The Good-examples block is the worst-affected section because it renders the canonical template content verbatim — every removed field is visible there, the launcher invocation is rendered with the wrong package name, and even the Bad-examples block carries `connector: obsidian` in its negative-counterexample shape. This is the narrative-section-escapes-rewrites pattern: a frontmatter-and-outcomes-scoped revision routinely leaves Good/Bad example blocks with stale references.

What would have caught this earlier: a `/aide:align` pass at the moment the root settled the schema collapse. The cascade is mechanical and the affected references are localized to the rendered-template blocks; the structural rules (seed-semantic idempotency, no `would-overwrite` for user-owned config, derivation through parseBrainAide + interpolateArgs) are all unchanged.

Two layers of drift in this spec:

1. **Architectural-shift drift (current):** Folder rename and new
   consumer (CLI) should have triggered a `/aide:spec` pass. The
   "init orchestrator" / "calling agent" terminology hardcodes the
   MCP-tool consumer.

2. **Prior cycle (resolved):** The seed-semantic / canonical-template
   idempotency distinction was a deliberate design pivot that landed
   cleanly. The spec's Strategy section codified the two-idempotency-mode
   distinction explicitly and named the distinguishing question ("who
   owns the bytes after first write?"). The parameterized regression
   test (`describe.each` over four on-disk states) is the right
   regression anchor for a data-loss-class invariant.

Stage to watch going forward: the prior cycle introduced placeholder
wikilinks (`[[your-conventions-note]]`, `[[your-folder-structure-note]]`)
and a negative assertion in the test. Any future edit to
`PLAYBOOK_HUB_TEMPLATE` that adds named wikilinks (even well-intentioned
ones) will trip the negative assertion. The spec's
`outcomes.undesired[1]` is the first place a future implementor should
look when that assertion fails.
